  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Definition",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply the Fourier transform definition to convert an aperiodic continuous-time signal $x(t)$ into its continuous-frequency spectrum $X(j \\omega)$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Definition",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fourier Transform Definition",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Periodic continuous-time signals repeat with a finite period and are naturally described by Fourier series. An aperiodic signal does not repeat, so the forward Fourier transform gives its continuous-frequency description in terms of $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read the definition term by term: $x(t)$ is the time-domain signal, $e^{-j\\omega t}$ is the complex exponential that probes frequency $\\omega$, and the integral adds the contribution from every time instant. The result $X(j\\omega)$ is the continuous-frequency spectrum of $x(t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\begin{cases}1, & -T/2 \\le t \\le T/2\\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a compact-support signal like this rectangular pulse, the integral is still written over all time, but the zero-valued regions contribute nothing. The calculation therefore depends only on the support of $x(t)$, which is the first thing to notice before any later example evaluates $X(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Definition",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Substituting a Signal into the Fourier Transform Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $x(t)=1$ for $-T/2 \\le t \\le T/2$ and $0$ otherwise, write the Fourier transform integral for $X(j\\omega)$ and substitute the signal into the integrand.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the forward Fourier transform definition. Start from $X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$ and keep the infinite-time limits, because this step only inserts the signal into the integrand.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the signal is the compact-support pulse from the prompt: $x(t)=1$ on $-T/2 \\le t \\le T/2$ and $0$ otherwise. Replace $x(t)$ by that piecewise form directly inside the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\begin{cases}1, & -T/2 \\le t \\le T/2\\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{cases} e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the pure substitution result. The integrand now contains the chosen $x(t)$, but the bounds are still $-\\infty$ to $\\infty$; the next section will reduce them to the support interval.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Definition",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Setting Integration Limits from Aperiodic Signal Support",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Using the same pulse, identify the nonzero time interval and rewrite $X(j\\omega)$ with the correct integration limits.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the pulse support from the prompt: $x(t)$ is nonzero only on $-T/2 \\le t \\le T/2$. So the Fourier transform integral should keep only that interval when we set the bounds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $x(t)=0$ outside that support, the infinite limits can be replaced by the nonzero interval without changing the transform. Keep the integrand the same and only shrink the bounds.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-T/2}^{T/2} e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the bounded setup we wanted. The support has collapsed the full-time integral to the exact finite interval where $x(t)$ lives, and the next step will evaluate the result in terms of $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Definition",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Evaluating $X(j \\omega)$ as a Function of Continuous Frequency",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-T/2}^{T/2} e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$ and simplify the result into a closed-form expression.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the bounded Fourier transform integral from the previous step. Integrate $e^{-j\\omega t}$ with respect to $t$, then evaluate the antiderivative at the two limits to turn the transform into a function of $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}X(j\\omega) &= \\int_{-T/2}^{T/2} e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt \\\\ &= \\left[\\frac{e^{-j\\omega t}}{-j\\omega}\\right]_{-T/2}^{T/2} \\\\ &= \\frac{e^{-j\\omega T/2}-e^{j\\omega T/2}}{-j\\omega}\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now simplify the exponential difference into a trigonometric form. That keeps the result as a continuous-frequency spectrum and makes the dependence on $\\omega$ easy to read.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}X(j\\omega) &= \\frac{e^{-j\\omega T/2}-e^{j\\omega T/2}}{-j\\omega} \\\\ &= \\frac{2\\sin(\\omega T/2)}{\\omega}\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the closed-form transform is $X(j\\omega)=\\frac{2\\sin(\\omega T/2)}{\\omega}$. At $\\omega=0$, the removable limit is $X(0)=T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform Definition",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting the Transform Result as a Continuous-Frequency Spectrum",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given the closed-form $X(j\\omega)$ from the previous step, explain what it says about the signal’s continuous-frequency spectrum and the role of $\\omega$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the closed-form $X(j\\omega)$ from the previous step. Read it as a function of continuous frequency: every real $\\omega$ gives one value, so the result is a continuous spectrum rather than isolated harmonics or a discrete coefficient list.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=\\frac{2\\sin(\\omega T/2)}{\\omega}, \\qquad X(0)=T",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The value at $\\omega=0$ is the zero-frequency component, and here it equals the pulse area. That tells you how much DC content the finite pulse carries.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $x(t)$ is supported on a finite time interval, its transform is spread across a range of $\\omega$ instead of living at one frequency index. The shape of $X(j\\omega)$ is therefore a continuous-frequency spectrum whose width and sidelobes come from the time-limited pulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
